      {
        "id": 94188,
        "name": "Edwin",
        "username": "Edwin",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/73ab20/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2014-03-10T01:37:09.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Ameena\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/ameena/48/1907_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Ameena:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Does the Skydeck thingy say \"When a target would be destroyed, put it to the bottom of its deck instead\"? Because if so, that's a bit like Unforgiving Wasteland's \"When a target would be destroyed (by the environment), remove it from the game instead\" which lets you get around stuff like Lilian's Fowl-destruction damage. But I suppose if it says \"when a target  is  destroyed…\" (as opposed to \"would be destroyed\"), then it still does its normal destruction stuff, just goes to the bottom of the deck afterwards rather than the trash.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Unfortunately, now that I've read it, it provides no such clarification.&nbsp; It can't ever work on One-Shots, of course, since those aren't \"destroyed\" but simply \"put into trash\".&nbsp; But if we pretend that it could be in play at the same time as Imbued Vitality, then all other cards would be targets and thus would be Skydecked \"when destroyed\", which seems to imply that they are still indeed destroyed.&nbsp; But I still wish it had been written with \"instead of into the trash\" for clarity's sake, and for that matter that it had said \"on the bottom of that deck\" instead of \"underneath that deck\" (which implies that there's some Dreamer-esque limbo zone underneath the deck, which wouldn't count as part of the deck itself).&nbsp; Especially given that Parse and Writhe have cards which mess with the bottom cards of decks, and the use of different language on Skydeck seems to indicate a distinction, one that'd be meant to prevent Writhe from Extracting a Skydecked target, but probably doesn't actually exist.</p>\n<p>Would it be possible for someone who playtested for Vengeance to shed some light on this weirdness?</p>",
        "post_number": 22,
